  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nUpper level ridge of high pressure has finally started it \neastward slide as an approaching upper level trough digs across \nthe Pacific NW into California. In between these two features \nsoutherly flow will continue to bring monsoonal moisture over the \nSilver State.  \n\nWednesday through Friday, the upper level trough will move \nthrough Nevada. Models show that this system will help Nevada out\nin multiple ways, first dropping temperatures significantly \nthrough the end of the week with highs returning to the low 80s to\nlow 90s. Second, it will help channel monsoonal moisture flow \nincreasing chances for wetter storm modes. Finally, the added \ndynamic cooling and dynamic lift will also help to increase \noverall precipitation chances. The result will be a 30% to 80% \nchance for isolated to scattered afternoon and evening \nthunderstorms and showers. The highest probability will remain \nacross central Nevada, but chances have risen to 40% to 60% of \nthunderstorms for northern Nevada. Models are now showing better \nchances for all of northern Nevada to see precipitation this week.\nThere still is a possibility that NW Humboldt county gets missed,\nbut model consensus QPF now shows up to 0.25\u201d of rainfall through\nSaturday morning with ares of central NV could receive up to \n0.75\u201d or more. \n\nAfter Saturday the first upper trough lifts NE into Canada which \nmay lower storm chances a bit over the weekend, but storm chances \nwill average 20% to 40%. Next Monday through Wednesday Models have\nbeen inconsistent with the evolution of the upper pattern, and \ntoday\u2019s solutions are no different. GFS and the ECMWF ensembles \nhave been trading solutions of either another upper trough \nsomewhere over the western US, or the return of the upper level \nridge to the four corners or great basin. The one new agreement \nfor the most recent runs is keeping the southerly monsoonal flow. \nHowever, given the particular placement of either ridge or trough \nwill dictate how high or low storm and shower chances become for \nthe longer term. So for now will hold over a low 20% to 30% chance\nfor isolated thunderstorms mainly fir central and eastern NV for \nthe start of next week. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n\nThere is high confidence for chances of isolated to scattered wet\nwith a few dry thunderstorms Through Saturday afternoon for \ncentral Nevada. \n\nThere moderate confidence for isolated to scattered mix of wet \nand dry thunderstorms expanding to cover Humboldt and Elko \ncounties Wednesday through Saturday. \n\nThere is low confidence in the evolution of the upper pattern \nafter Sunday as models are disagreement as to the strength and \nlocation of key upper level features.",
